WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.940
ChatGPT just talked a company into waving a big cancellation fee during a support chat. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 194. Someone on Reddit needed out of Adobe Creative Cloud, a paid software plan with no clear cancel button. They asked the chatbot called ChatGPT for help, and it offered to handle a live support chat with Adobe. Artificial Intelligence, or ai, here worked as a teammate sitting beside them at the keyboard. The person pasted each suggested line in

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.260
to Adobe's window, one message at a time.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:37.260
Chat GPT stayed polite and firm, and it asked for three specific things.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:42.900
It wanted the exact cancellation date, any fees, and whether Adobe would drop the penalty

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.060
as a goodwill waiver.

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:49.520
A goodwill waiver is a company choosing to cancel a charge as a kindness, not because

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:50.600
a rule forces them.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:55.700
An early termination fee is extra money some firms charge if you leave before a contract

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:56.180
ends.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:00.660
A support chat can feel like a maze with the exit sign tucked behind a plant.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:05.620
The chatbot held the map, and it whispered the next turn so no promise got dropped.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:11.960
After several back and forth messages, Adobe waved about $359 Australian dollars in early

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:12.900
termination fees.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:15.100
Then they completed the cancellation.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:20.920
One suggested line asked, before going further, whether Adobe could drop that fee as a good

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:21.580
will exception.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:25.460
Another asked them to confirm the exact date access would end.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:31.700
It also asked whether any further charges would apply beyond the quoted $394 trillion in

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:32.700
99 cents.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:36.500
Adobe's replies referred back to the current subscription details.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:41.940
An agent then said that as a one-time exception, they had waived the early termination fee.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:47.160
Adobe closed the license count to zero, which means permission to keep using the software

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:48.000
was fully ended.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.740
They also promised no new renewal charges.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:57.520
images. The apps kept working through the next bill on October 31, 2026. The user said

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:02.440
chatGPT wrote every line except the last thank you, and called the result mind-blowing.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.640
Bringing the chat bot along is a bit like taking a calm, older sibling to a stressful call.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.680
You still tap send, but you stop freezing up or forgetting what the agent already promised.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:17.640
Lots of teens and students carry streaming or software plans that hide the way out.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:23.220
Using AI as a coach for those chats can save real money, not just help with school essays.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:28.560
The next time a company adds an extra charge, you can ask a chatbot to draft clear messages

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:29.120
for you.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:30.680
The experiment is free.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:34.860
Open chat GPT on your phone and describe any subscription problem.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:37.900
Ask it to write the first support message you should send.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:42.480
Paste those suggestions and leave that chat open so it can help with the next reply.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:47.080
memory is the hidden piece. Long group chats on your phone already carry a

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:51.520
history. You do not reintroduce yourself every afternoon because yesterday's

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:56.120
messages are still sitting there. An AI chat works from a similar pile of history.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:01.280
Picture every line you have ever sent it sitting in one giant notebook. Days or

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:06.060
weeks later it can flip those pages to recall what you like, what you asked, and

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:10.500
tiny details from older chats. The company that built the chatbot chooses how

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:15.960
long that notebook stays on its own computers. Sometimes that window is 30 days. Sometimes

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:20.840
it stretches to 5 years. First you type and those words land on the company's machines.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:26.340
Second they get stored as that notebook. Third a setting decides whether those pages

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:31.800
also help teach later versions of the chatbot. Fourth the next time you return the software

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:37.800
can peek at earlier pages before it answers. A virtual private network or VPN is a tool

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:42.680
that hides your Internet address. That is like wearing a disguise on the walk to the post office.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.920
The disguise can hide where you are standing. It cannot stop anyone from keeping the letter once

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:53.280
it arrives. The words you typed are already in the envelope. Changing the setting inside the app

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:59.120
itself matters more than any extra privacy gadget you add from the outside. The system is not magically

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:03.260
reading your mind. It is looking back through a notebook you filled with your own messages.

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:07.860
pages. Those stored pages are the whole trick behind a chatbot that seems to remember you.

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:12.120
You do not need a computer science class to follow that. You already live it in every

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:17.360
long text thread. You can change this today. Claude is another popular chatbot, and it

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:22.360
stores typical chats as long as five years unless you change that. That is much longer than

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:27.300
many people expect. In account preferences, you can cut that down so chats are kept only

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:33.000
30 days. Open the Claude website or app. Tap your name and find the toggle about whether

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.480
other chats can be used for training. Training here just means your words may help teach

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:42.060
a future version of the chatbot. Flip that switch off when you want the shorter window.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:47.680
If you pay for a business plan, tighter privacy rules apply from the start. Plenty of people

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:53.580
never clock that jump from 5 years down to 30 days until they open the menu. Chat GPT hides

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:59.880
a similar choice. Open Chat GPT, go to settings, then data controls. Switch off the control

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:04.540
that uses your chats to improve the model, which is the trained software doing the predicting.

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:10.360
Watch the toggle actually move. That small click is you deciding how long your notebook stays

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:15.640
on their computers. In South Korea, hiring ads now name Claude, where they used to ask for

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:22.500
computer skills. Those mentions rose 170% over a single year. Handling a chat bot is starting

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:28.120
to count as a core workplace skill, the way naming a specific tool in a listing beats a vague line

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:33.160
about computers. And that's a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go, huh, that's

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:39.140
cool. Go play with it. Curiosity is how every expert started. See you tomorrow. A rating

